course: dictionary
lesson: 14230
title: Narrowness: width and closed minds
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Narrow means not wide. Narrowness is the noun for this: it is the state of being narrow. You can use it to talk about how thin a space is, or about how small a range of ideas is. It is an uncountable noun, so we say "the narrowness", not "a narrowness".
explain: «The narrowness of the lane meant that only one car could pass at a time.» The clues are only one car could pass. That means the lane has very little width, so narrowness must mean being very narrow.
explain: Narrowness can also be about thinking. «His narrowness of mind stopped him from trying anything new.» He would not listen to other ideas. Ask: what word would fit here? Try "closed" or "small". The sentence still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Narrowness is a noun. Its adjective is narrow.
fact: «Narrowness» can mean having little width, as in «the narrowness of the bridge».
fact: «Narrowness» can also mean not being open to new ideas, as in «narrowness of mind».
fact: Stories often use narrowness to make a place feel tight and uncomfortable, as if the walls are closing in.
fact: Narrow is the adjective, so you can say «the road is narrow» and «the narrowness of the road».
exercise: «The narrowness of the bridge made the lorry driver slow right down.» What does narrowness mean here? | *Having little width from side to side | Being very long and tall | Being very loud | Having lots of bright paint
exercise: «His narrowness of mind meant he would not listen to anyone else.» What does narrowness of mind mean? | Having a very big imagination | *Not being open to other ideas | Thinking very quickly | Being good at maths
exercise: «The road is narrow, and its narrowness makes driving hard.» Copy the word that is the noun, meaning the state of being narrow. | driving | road | *narrowness | narrow
test: «The narrowness of the canal meant the boat could only just squeeze through.» What does narrowness describe here? | The speed of the water | The colour of the boat | The number of people on board | *The width of the canal
test: Ella says, «Her narrowness of thinking made the group stop discussing.» Which phrase is closest in meaning to narrowness here? | Bravery and courage | Kindness and warmth | Speed and energy | *A closed mind
exam: «The walls of the tunnel seemed to close in, and the narrowness made Sam feel uneasy.» What feeling does the narrowness of the tunnel create? | Excitement and joy | Sleepiness and calm | A happy surprise | *A tight, uncomfortable feeling
